      "id": "ut5vbqf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Himalayan Mountain System"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Himalayas are young fold mountains formed by the tectonic collision of the Indian and Eurasian plates.",
        "Kanchenjunga (8,586 m) in Sikkim is the highest mountain peak located within India.",
        "The Trans-Himalayan system comprises the Karakoram, Ladakh, and Zaskar ranges, housing K2 (Godwin-Austen).",
        "Anamudi (2,695 m) in the Anaimalai Hills is the highest peak in Peninsular India.",
        "The Nilgiri Hills serve as the geographic junction where the Western Ghats and Eastern Ghats meet."
      ],
      "id": "8l0ej8o"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rbwmnqy",
      "content": "The Himalayas are the youngest fold mountains in the world. They extend for about 2400 km from Jammu &amp; Kashmir in the west to Arunachal Pradesh in the east. The Himalayas were formed due to the collision between the Indian Plate and the Eurasian Plate."
    },
