    {
      "id": "f7c2fzg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Importance of Mountains in India"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ao0ctyc",
      "content": "• Mountains act as natural barriers and protect the country."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x9mrz0g",
      "content": "• They influence the climate and monsoon rainfall."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mx7a0w5",
      "content": "• They are the source of major rivers."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ksy9q0r",
      "content": "• They provide forests, biodiversity and natural resources."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2ltzk3x",
      "content": "• They support tourism and hill stations."
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "K2 (Godwin-Austen) belongs to the Karakoram Range (Trans-Himalayas), whereas Kanchenjunga belongs to the Great Himalayas.",
        "Passes in Western Ghats: Thal Ghat connects Mumbai to Nashik; Bhor Ghat connects Mumbai to Pune; Pal Ghat connects Palakkad to Coimbatore.",
        "Western Ghats are continuous and cause heavy orographic rainfall, while Eastern Ghats are discontinuous and dissected by major east-flowing rivers."
      ],
      "id": "8x9invf"
    },
